    faqs: [{"q":"How should a freelance writer bill — per word, per hour, or per project?","a":"Bill however you agreed with the client. Per-word rates are common for articles and copy, per-hour suits research or editing, and flat project fees work for defined deliverables like a landing page. This template handles all three: put the rate in the rate column and the unit (words, hours, or a flat fee) in the description, and the line total does the math."},{"q":"Do I need a registered business or LLC to invoice as a writer?","a":"No. In the US a sole proprietor can invoice under their own legal name — you don't need an LLC or a registered business to bill a client. Add a business name later if you form one. This is general information, not tax or legal advice."},{"q":"What payment terms should I put on a writing invoice?","a":"Net 15 or Net 30 is standard, meaning payment is due 15 or 30 days after the invoice date. Put the terms and the exact due date in the notes so there's no confusion, and if you charge a late fee or a kill fee, state it there too."},{"q":"How do I number my invoices if I write for several clients?","a":"Use one running sequence across all clients, or add a short client code as a prefix (for example ACME-001, ACME-002). Either way, never reuse an invoice number — every invoice needs its own, even one you void or cancel — so your records and any client's accounts stay clean."}],
